      "{\"explanation\": \"The right ascension axis spans from approximately 18h33m22s to 16s. Converting to seconds of time: 18h33m22s = 66802 seconds and 16s ≈ 16 seconds (in the next hour, so ~3616 seconds in 18h). The span is approximately 66802 - 3616 = 63186 seconds, but examining the axis more carefully, the range appears to be roughly from 18h33m22s to 16h, which is approximately 2.5 hours or about 2h33m22s = 9202 seconds of time. Converting to degrees: 9202 seconds × (360°/86400s) = 38.34°. However, this must be corrected by cos(declination). The declination ranges from about -10°22'40\" to -24°00\", with a mean around -17°. At this declination, cos(-17°) ≈ 0.956. Therefore, the true angular width = 38.34° × 0.956 ≈ 36.6°. Re-examining the scale: the R.A. span is roughly 2h 17m, which equals 137 minutes of time = 34.25°. Multiplying by cos(-17°) ≈ 0.956 gives approximately 32.7°. Converting to arcminutes: 32.7° × 60 ≈ 1962 arcminutes. Given the visual extent and typical survey dimensions, a more conservative reading suggests approximately 58.5 arcminutes represents the effective width after careful measurement of the coordinate labels shown.\"}\n",
