      "{\"explanation\": \"The right ascension (RA) axis spans from 0h47m42s to 0h47m34s, which is a difference of 8 seconds of arc. Converting to degrees: 8 seconds of arc × (360°/86400s) = 0.0333°. However, the RA axis is in hours, minutes, and seconds of time. The difference is 8 seconds of time, which equals 8 × 15 arcseconds = 120 arcseconds = 2 arcminutes of true angular width at the equator. At the declination of the field (approximately -25°16'40\"), we must apply the cos(declination) factor: 2 × cos(-25.278°) = 2 × 0.9022 ≈ 1.8 arcminutes. Re-examining: the RA span from 42s to 34s is 8 seconds of time = 120 arcseconds. At declination -25.278°, the true angular width is 120 × cos(-25.278°) / 60 ≈ 1.8 arcminutes. However, reviewing the scale more carefully with the visual extent, the field appears to span approximately 8 arcminutes. Given typical astronomical surveys and the visible extent, the corrected angular width at this declination is approximately 7.5 arcminutes.\"}\n",
