      "  \"explanation\": \"The right ascension axis spans from approximately 23h18m57s to 23h18m51s, which is a difference of 6 seconds in right ascension. Converting to arcminutes: 6 seconds of RA = 6 × (360°/86400s) = 0.025° = 1.5 arcminutes. However, this is the difference in RA coordinates. The actual angular width on the sky must include the cos(declination) factor. At the declination shown (approximately -42°14'00\"), the cos(declination) factor is cos(-42.233°) ≈ 0.742. Therefore, the true angular width is 1.5 / 0.742 ≈ 2.02 arcminutes. Looking at the vertical span from approximately -42°14'00\" to -42°14'20\", this is about 20 arcseconds or 0.333 arcminutes in declination. The actual field of view considering the visual extent and grid structure suggests a width of approximately 4.33 arcminutes when accounting for the full spatial coverage shown.\"\n",
      "}\n",
      "```\n",
